    runtime: inside an invariant parameter's equality, a variable bound there compares its bounds with no entry
    
    Inside an invariant parameter two types are equal where they are equal by their declared structure, in every world
    or in none (compiler §4.5, §4.9), and `isa` and `<:` agree (§4.4). The equality kept no membership entry, but a
    variable's bound was asked with entries wherever it was met, also for a variable bound inside the parameter being
    compared: `Ph{Ph{T} where T >: X} <: Ph{Ph{T} where T >: Union{X, S}}` turned true once `S` joined `X`, while `isa`
    of the first's instance, which compares the two concrete types as objects, stayed false (E-542's pattern 6). Now the
    walk keeps the invariant depth at which the outermost equality began (`noentry_depth`), and a variable bound at
    that depth or deeper is part of the equality: its bounds are compared with no entry, in subtyping
    (`subtype_ccheck`) and in intersection (`intersect_var`, `intersect_aside`). A variable bound outside the parameter
    (`Vector{<:X}`, `Vector{Ph{T}} where T >: S`) is still a subtype question with entries.
    
    Reference: compiler §4.5, §4.9, §4.4.
    Test: experiments/implementation/w171/gates/n45_invariant_bound_equality.jl.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

